(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do natural killer cells kill?","choices":["Only extracellular parasites","Cells infected by viruses","Free bacteria in plasma","Only cancer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cytotoxic against infected cells. Bacteria or protozoan parasites. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is the therapeutic window?","choices":["The interval between doses at which plasma concentration falls below the minimal inhibitory concentration","The period during which a drug remains detectable in serum","The range of plasma concentrations spanning the minimum concentration for clinical efficacy and toxicity","The range of organisms against which a drug is effective"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The therapeutic index is then the calculation used to assess safety and efficacy against that window. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does antibody defeat the sialic acid evasion strategy?","choices":["Antibody prevents C5 cleavage","Antibody masks the sialic acid first","Antibody cleaves sialic acid enzymatically","Antibody inactivates factor H directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — so these bacteria resist complement ONLY when no specific antibody is present. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What defines a nosocomial infection?","choices":["One acquired or developing during a hospital stay","Any infection resistant to antibiotics","Any infection in an immunocompromised patient","An infection present on admission"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, also called a hospital-acquired infection. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does the unit CFU stand for, and why is it used?","choices":["Colony fraction unit — a proportion of the plate","Colony-forming unit","Cell freezing unit — a measure of viability","Culture fluid unit — a measure of volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A visible colony may have started from one cell or a thousand, so the count is of colonies rather than cells. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are the three stated functions of the glycocalyx?","choices":["Storage of phosphate, oil droplets and waste products","Involvement in biofilm production, prevention of desiccation, and prevention of phagocytosis","Crosslinking peptidoglycan, retaining crystal violet, and resisting osmotic stress","Conjugation, motility, and adhesion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Preventing phagocytosis is called out as a factor of pathogenicity, with Streptococcus pneumoniae as the example. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which factor weakens host defences?","choices":["Immunosuppressing drugs and organ transplants","A naturally low infectious dose for the organism","Presence of resident flora","A high morbidity rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside age, genetic defects, stress and chronic disease. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the secondary response also called?","choices":["The innate response","The passive response","The anamnestic response","The primary response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which cytokine besides tumour necrosis factor alpha is named as increasing temperature?","choices":["Interleukin 6","CXCL8","Interferon beta","Interleukin 12"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How do integrase inhibitors work?","choices":["They inhibit uncoating of the virion","They prevent the viral genes from entering the host chromosome, so the viral DNA is ultimately degraded","They block the proteolytic cleavage of viral polypeptide precursors into mature proteins","They prevent the virus escaping the host cell to infect others"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After reverse transcription the integrase forms a stable complex with viral DNA and mediates integration; blocking it leaves the DNA unintegrated. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"How does fever affect tissue resistance to tumour necrosis factor alpha?","choices":["It has no effect on it","It converts it into interleukin 6","It ENHANCES resistance to its damaging effects","It reduces resistance, worsening damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a protective effect against the body's own mediator. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What two cell types arise from B-cell clonal expansion?","choices":["Macrophages and dendritic cells","Stromal cells and stem cells","Plasma cells that secrete antibody","Cytotoxic and helper T cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What happens to the genes encoding the variable region once a B cell is committed?","choices":["They continue to rearrange with each division","They are discarded after the first antigen encounter","They are replaced by the antigen's own genes","They are locked in for the life of the cell and its progeny"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — including its memory cells and activated descendants. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Cephalosporins were originally isolated from which organism, and how does their generation ordering work?","choices":["Cephalosporium acremonium; first generation narrow spectrum and Gram-positive, second extended to include Gram-negative, third including pseudomonads and injected, fourth oral","Streptomyces griseus; first generation broad spectrum and Gram-negative, second narrowed to Gram-positive, third oral, fourth injected against pseudomonads","Penicillium chrysogenum; all generations are equivalent in spectrum","Bacillus subtilis; later generations are topical only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Their mechanism is similar to the transpeptidase activity of penicillin. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Beta-lactamases and aminoglycoside modifying enzymes are examples of which resistance mechanism?","choices":["Bypass of target","Drug inactivation by enzymes","Increased efflux","Modification of the target site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is one of the routes named in the definition — destroying the antibiotic. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Echinocandins differ from azoles in which way?","choices":["Both act on the fungal membrane, differing only in potency","Echinocandins are reserved for azole-resistant infections; azoles are the agents typically given by the intravenous route in serious systemic disease","Echinocandins block synthesis of beta-glucan, an essential component of the fungal CELL WALL; azoles inhibit ergosterol synthesis, acting on the MEMBRANE","Echinocandins inhibit ergosterol synthesis; azoles block beta-glucan synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Echinocandins are lipopeptides naturally produced by some fungal species, given intravenously, and used against Candida and Pneumocystis. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which is an example of a sequela?","choices":["Blindness from gonococcal conjunctivitis","Fever during acute infection","A positive blood culture","Cough during a respiratory infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside polio paralysis and Lyme arthritis. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How does fever act against BACTERIA specifically?","choices":["It blocks their adhesion to mucosa","It starves them of iron","It denatures their ribosomes","It lyses their cell walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — iron starvation is the antibacterial mechanism. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which everyday test demonstrates droplet spread through a face covering?","choices":["Holding a tissue in front of the mouth","Counting breaths per minute","Trying to blow out a candle while wearing one","Fogging a pair of glasses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the candle test. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which everyday hospital items are named among the electronic fomites?","choices":["Mops and brooms","Telephones and keyboards","Scalpels and syringes","Bed rails and gurneys"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Escherichia coli uses type I fimbriae to adhere to which receptor, facilitating colonisation of which site?","choices":["Mannose receptors; the urinary tract","Toll-like receptors; the gut epithelium","Sialic acid receptors; the respiratory tract","Complement receptors; the bloodstream"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the adhesion mechanism behind urinary tract colonisation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which three techniques fall under genetic and molecular analysis?","choices":["Guanine plus cytosine base composition, deoxyribonucleic acid analysis using genetic probes, and nucleic acid sequencing with ribosomal RNA analysis","Colony appearance on selective agar, motility testing in semisolid medium, and spore staining with capsule staining under the light microscope","Gram staining, culture, and colony counting","Serology, biochemistry, and chemical analysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three sit under the genetic and molecular heading. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do Streptococcus pyogenes and Staphylococcus aureus evade complement?","choices":["They shed the membrane attack complex","They block the classical pathway only","They coat themselves in sialic acid","They secrete an enzyme that digests C3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they mimic the human cell surface. Which binds factor H so their C3b is inactivated. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How long does PASSIVE immunisation typically protect for?","choices":["Lifelong","About two weeks","About five years","Two to three months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — short-term protection from donated antibody. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is an antigenic determinant also called, and what is it?","choices":["A titre — a measure of antibody concentration","An epitope — a small molecular group recognised by lymphocytes","A hapten — a small molecule that is not antigenic alone","An adjuvant — a substance that boosts a response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and one antigen may carry many of them. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pairing of aminoglycoside to producing organism is correct?","choices":["Streptomycin from Streptomyces fradiae; neomycin from Micromonospora purpurea; gentamycin from Streptomyces griseus","All three are produced by Streptomyces griseus","All three are produced synthetically from coal tar dyes","Streptomycin from Streptomyces griseus; neomycin from Streptomyces fradiae; gentamycin from Micromonospora purpurea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Note that gentamycin's producer is a Micromonospora rather than a Streptomyces. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed among the desirable characteristics of an antimicrobial agent?","choices":["Rapid development of resistance","Low potential for hypersensitive reaction","Favourable pharmacokinetics","Good therapeutic index"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. LITTLE resistance development is what is wanted; rapid development is the problem, not the goal. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which categories of toxin fall under toxigenicity?","choices":["Exoenzymes, leukocidins and antiphagocytic factors","Capsules and slimes","Fimbriae and flagella","Neurotoxins, enterotoxins, hemotoxins, nephrotoxins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, grouped by the tissue targeted. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is the most common non-human cell type in the body?","choices":["Fungi","Viruses","Protozoa","Bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which cells produce interferon ALPHA?","choices":["T cells","Hepatocytes","Lymphocytes and macrophages","Fibroblasts and epithelial cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which host factor matters most?","choices":["Geographic location","Immune status","Blood group","Height and weight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — immune status is the major one. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organisms were found at counts above ten thousand per millilitre?","choices":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Acinetobacter","Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes and Enterococcus","Candida and Aspergillus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — gram-negative organisms. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Penicillins interfere with the bacterial cell wall in three ways. Which set is correct?","choices":["Binding ergosterol, forming pores, and inducing reactive oxygen species","Inhibiting DNA gyrase, preventing unwinding, and blocking RNA polymerase","Binding penicillin-binding proteins, inhibiting transpeptidase, and producing autolysins","Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit, blocking translocation, and inhibiting peptide bond formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Autolysins are natural remodelling enzymes, and the damage comes from breakdown without accompanying synthesis. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How is rabies transmitted between humans, if at all?","choices":["Commonly, by respiratory droplet","Commonly, by sexual contact","Never, under any circumstance","Rarely, through organ transplantation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does a portal of exit describe?","choices":["How pathogens enter the host","Where a pathogen normally lives","How a pathogen adheres to tissue","How pathogens depart the host"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What defines a focal infection?","choices":["An agent breaks loose from a local site and spreads","Several microbes grow at one site","An infection confined to one tissue","A second infection following disruption of the flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Allergy is also known as which type of hypersensitivity?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What distinguishes Chlamydias from Rickettsias?","choices":["Chlamydias are free-living organisms, whereas Rickettsias are obligate intracellular pathogens","Chlamydias are NOT transmitted by arthropod vectors; Rickettsias are transmitted by fleas, ticks and lice","Chlamydias lack a cell wall; Rickettsias have one","Chlamydias are eukaryotic; Rickettsias are prokaryotic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are tiny obligate intracellular pathogens; vector transmission is the stated difference. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which internal bacterial structures are present?","choices":["Capsule, slime layer, and biofilm matrix","Flagella, pili and fimbriae","A defined nuclear membrane, mitochondria, and 80S ribosomes within the cytoplasm","Cytoplasm, organelles, a nuclear area with circular chromosome, 70S ribosomes, and inclusions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inclusions store products such as phosphate and oil droplets. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which cell works as infection begins, and which waits to be called?","choices":["The neutrophil works first and calls the macrophage","Both arrive simultaneously","The dendritic cell works first and calls both","The macrophage works first and raises the alarm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil waits for it to enter tissue. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What do ALL T cells produce?","choices":["Complement proteins","Histamine","Cytokines","Antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the helper cells especially, sending chemical messages to T cells, B cells and phagocytes. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Bee or wasp venom belongs in which allergen category?","choices":["Ingested","Contact","Injected","Inhaled"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside some medications. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which route counts as indirect contact?","choices":["Airborne droplet nuclei","Kissing","Transplacental spread","A tick bite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with vehicles and fomites. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between a disinfectant and an antiseptic?","choices":["Disinfectants are chemical; antiseptics are physical","Disinfectants are used on inanimate objects; antiseptics are used on tissues","Disinfectants sterilise completely; antiseptics do not affect microbes at all","Disinfectants are naturally produced; antiseptics are synthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lysol is the disinfectant, Listerine mouthwash the antiseptic. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Decimal reduction time is defined as which of the following?","choices":["The time taken for the culture to enter the stationary phase","The time taken for a population to double","The time taken to kill 90% of the organisms","The time taken to kill 100% of the organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the death rate is constant, each successive interval removes another 90% of what remains. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does transformation contribute to antibiotic resistance?","choices":["A plasmid carrying the resistance gene is passed directly between two cells during conjugation","A transposon moves between a plasmid and the chromosome","Resistance genes are acquired from a recently killed cell and incorporated into a chromosome or plasmid","A bacteriophage carries resistance genes between bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which means killing a resistant organism can still leave its resistance genes available to survivors. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is chemotherapy?","choices":["The use of microbial products to stimulate host immunity","The combination of two or more drugs to overcome resistance","The treatment of malignancy with cytotoxic agents","The use of drugs to treat a disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antimicrobial drugs are then the subset that interferes with microbial growth within a host. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where do encounters between host defenses and invaders take place?","choices":["The reticuloendothelial system","The bloodstream only","Inside epithelial cells only","The bone marrow and thymus only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the four compartments named. Extracellular fluid, bloodstream and lymphatic system. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Under which environmental conditions is influenza virus more stable and transmissible?","choices":["Low temperatures and high humidity","High temperatures and high humidity","Low temperatures and low humidity","High temperatures and low humidity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low temperature with high humidity favours influenza stability and transmission. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which reservoir is shared by Ebola, Marburg and Nipah viruses?","choices":["Fruit bats","Birds","Rodents","Domestic cattle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with small primates for Ebola. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why is horizontal gene transfer by conjugation so significant for resistance?","choices":["It requires a bacteriophage","It only transfers chromosomal DNA","Species need not match","It only occurs between identical strains"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and every daughter cell inherits the plasmid. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What characterises the lag phase of the bacterial growth curve?","choices":["Rate of cell growth equalling rate of cell death","Exponential death of cells in their own wastes","Maximum growth, sustained while nutrients and environment remain favourable","A flat period of adjustment and enlargement, with little growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cells are adapting rather than dividing. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does antibody lead to complement-mediated lysis?","choices":["It digests the cell envelope itself","It binds the bacterium","It converts directly into complement proteins","It blocks complement from reaching the cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is complement fixation in the specific immune response. Leaving sites for complement proteins to bind and use perforins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What did the birthday-cake authors NOT do?","choices":["Use a control condition","Report colony counts","Publish their results","Identify the bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the organisms were counted but never identified. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the MOST prevalent, and the only one crossing the placenta?","choices":["IgA","IgM","IgE","IgG"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is responsible for long-term immunity. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How does interleukin 1 beta escape the macrophage, and what happens to the cell?","choices":["It is exported by vesicle and the macrophage survives","It diffuses freely across the membrane","It is released only after phagocytosis","Through pores; the cell dies by pyroptosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — release costs the cell its life. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which illness is the commonest consequence of FOOD as a transmission vehicle?","choices":["Gastrointestinal illness","Respiratory illness","Skin infection","Central nervous system infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hence food safety and quality. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What did Lister use in the 1860s?","choices":["Silver nitrate","Carbolic acid","Chlorine solution","Alcohol hand gel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Bacterial growth refers to an increase in what, and occurs by which process?","choices":["Increase in cell size, by binary fission","Increase in cell number, by conjugation","Increase in colony diameter, by sporulation","Increase in cell number, by binary fission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Growth means number rather than size of the cell, and binary fission gives exponential rather than arithmetic growth. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is a disease with occasional cases at irregular intervals called?","choices":["Epidemic","Pandemic","Sporadic","Endemic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 56"}]};
